## Drag & drop

| CDK | dnd-kit |
|---|---|
| `CdkDropListGroup` on the shell | one `<DndContext>` in `BuilderShell`, `PointerSensor` only, with `activationConstraint: { distance: 5 }`. No `KeyboardSensor`: Enter and Space on a palette item or an actions button open its menu |
| palette `cdkDrag` + `cdkDropListHasAnchor` + `acceptNothing` | `useDraggable` per palette `<li>`, rendered through `<DragOverlay>` so the original stays put; the palette registers no droppable |
| cell `cdkDropList` + `cdkDropListOrientation="mixed"` + `[cdkDropListData]` | `useDroppable({ id, data: cell })` wrapping a `<SortableContext>` of the cell's uids with `rectSortingStrategy` (spans sit side by side, so a vertical-list strategy is wrong) |
| element `cdkDrag` | `useSortable({ id: uid, data: element })` on the wrapper `<div>` |
| `(cdkDropListDropped)` → `Canvas.drop` | `onDragEnd` → pure `resolveDrop(active, over, cells)` in `canvas-drop.ts` returning `{ kind: 'add' \| 'move', cell, index }`, then `addElement` / `moveElement` |
| `.cdk-drop-list-receiving` / `-dragging` in `styles.css` | `isOver` / `isDragging` from the hooks → conditional classes, including `z-10` on the hovered cell (cells overlap their neighbours' borders) |
| `.cdk-drag-animating` only under `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`; `motion-reduce:transition-none` on cells | a `usePrefersReducedMotion()` hook (`matchMedia`): `dropAnimation={null}` on `DragOverlay` and `transition: null` in `useSortable` when it is true; the cell class copies as is |
| `(mousedown)` / `(touchstart)` `stopPropagation()` on the actions button **and on every form control** | `onPointerDown={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}` on the same elements, shared as one `noDrag` props object |

Spread only `listeners` and `setNodeRef` from `useDraggable` / `useSortable`, not `attributes`. `attributes` adds `role="button"`, `tabIndex={0}`, `aria-roledescription` and `aria-describedby`, which would make every wrapper a tab stop and change the accessibility tree the tests read. Keyboard users get the menus below instead.

`resolveDrop` keeps the existing rule that only canvas elements carry `paletteUid`. Drop index: over an element → that element's index in its cell; over the cell itself → end of the cell. For a same-cell reorder this matches `moveElement`'s "index counted without the element itself".

Tests that pin drag behaviour beyond the basics:

- `form-elements.e2e.ts`: a drag that starts on the `<input>` must not move the element (and typing in it must work), while a drag that starts on the wrapper beside the control must move it.
- `drag-drop.e2e.ts`: "still drops and reorders elements when reduced motion is requested", and "lays dropped spans out next to each other and a div on a line of its own".
- `keyboard.e2e.ts`: "announces an element dropped with the pointer the same way" also asserts that no menu is open after a palette drag (see `ElementPalette`).

Cross-cell insertion preview (the gap CDK shows while hovering another list) needs an `onDragOver` projection held in local state. Do it after the e2e suite is green; it is polish, not part of the contract.

## Keyboard alternative and focus

Everything a drag can do is also reachable from a menu. Both paths call the same store actions, so the announcements are identical.

| Drag | Menu | Store call |
|---|---|---|
| palette item → cell | palette button "Add {label}" → menu "Add {label} to" → cell | `addElement(item, cell, cell.elements.length)` |
| reorder within a cell | actions menu → "Move earlier" / "Move later" | `moveElement(uid, ownCell, position ∓ 1)` |
| move to another cell | actions menu → "Move to cell" submenu → cell | `moveElement(uid, cell, cell.elements.length)` |

Focus rules, each pinned by `keyboard.e2e.ts`:

| After | Focus goes to | How in React |
|---|---|---|
| picking a cell from a palette menu, or dismissing it with Escape | the palette button | Base UI returns focus to the trigger by default |
| moving an element (within or across cells) | that element's actions button, in its new place | `DroppedElement` calls `onMoved()`; `Canvas` records the uid and focuses the button in an effect after the commit |
| saving or cancelling an edit dialog, or cancelling a deletion | the element's actions button | `finalFocus={actionsButtonRef}` on the dialog's `Popup`: the menu item that opened it is gone by then |
| confirming a deletion | the cell the element was in | `DroppedElement` calls `onRemoved()`; `Canvas` focuses the cell (`tabIndex={-1}`: focusable, not a tab stop) after the commit |

`Canvas` keeps two ref maps for this, actions buttons by uid and cell elements by `row:column`, plus one `pendingFocus` state consumed by a `useEffect`. An element that changes cell is unmounted and remounted (React cannot reparent), so its old button no longer exists and focus has to be placed explicitly. For a move the menu closes at the same moment and Base UI will try to return focus to the old trigger: if that lands after the effect, set `finalFocus={false}` on the actions menu's `Popup` while a move is pending. The Angular `canvas.spec.ts` case "leaves focus alone until then, as the dialog that asked is still handing it back" describes the same ordering for deletion: the cell is focused only after the removal has rendered.

