### `Canvas`

- A cell's classes are `canvas-drop-zone -mt-0.5 -ml-0.5 min-h-24 border-2 border-dashed border-base-300 bg-base-100 p-6 transition-colors motion-reduce:transition-none`. It is `flex flex-col` only while empty, to centre the "Drop elements here" hint. With elements it is plain block flow, which is what lets spans share a line.
- Passes each `DroppedElement` its `index` and `siblingCount` (it is already mapping over `cell.elements`), plus `onMoved`, `onRemoved` and the ref callback for its actions button. That keeps `DroppedElement` from subscribing to the whole `canvasElements` array just to find its own position.
- Owns the focus handling described above.

### `DroppedElement`

Six render branches. `HOVER_OUTLINE` is now `OUTLINE` and carries a `focus-within:` twin of every `hover:` class, so keyboard users get the same cue; it copies unchanged. The wrapper is `group relative mb-2 cursor-grab rounded-box border border-transparent p-1 pr-10` plus `mr-2 inline-block align-top` for a span and `block` for everything else. The type badge is `aria-hidden` and shows on `group-focus-within:` as well as `group-hover:`.

| Type | Renders | Test id | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `div` | `<div>` with text | `dropped-div` | |
| `span` | `<span>` with text | `dropped-span` | wrapper is inline-block |
| `text-input` | `<input type="text">`, `aria-label="Text input"` | `dropped-text-input` | `text` is the placeholder; controlled: `value={element.value}`, `onChange` → `updateValue`; `noDrag` |
| `textfield` | `<textarea>`, `aria-label="Textfield"` | `dropped-textfield` | same |
| `datepicker` | `<input type="date">`, `aria-label="Datepicker"` | `dropped-datepicker` | controlled the same way; `noDrag` |
| `dropdown` | `<select>`, `aria-label="Dropdown"` | `dropped-dropdown` | one `<option>` per entry, keyed by index; controlled: `value={element.value}`, `onChange` → `updateValue`; `noDrag` |

Control values are store state, not DOM state: an element is unmounted and remounted when it changes cell, and tests in `form-elements.e2e.ts` and `keyboard.e2e.ts` check that what was typed, picked or selected survives a move and a grid shrink.

Actions menu (Base UI `Menu`, the `Popup` carrying `aria-label={actionsLabel}`):

- "Edit text" when `'text' in element`; "Edit options" when `element.type === 'dropdown'`.
- "Move earlier" and "Move later" only when `siblingCount > 1`. "Move earlier" is disabled for the first element and "Move later" for the last, with DaisyUI's `menu-disabled` on the `<li>`. A disabled item must stay reachable with the arrow keys and pressing Enter on it must leave the menu open: `keyboard.e2e.ts` asserts `toBeFocused()` and `toBeDisabled()` on the same item. Confirm that Base UI's `disabled` on `Menu.Item` behaves this way (`aria-disabled`, still highlightable); if it removes the item from keyboard navigation, set `aria-disabled` and guard the handler by hand.
- "Move to cell" only when the grid has another cell: `Menu.SubmenuRoot` + `Menu.SubmenuTrigger`, opened with ArrowRight, listing every cell but the element's own. Picking a cell closes both menus.
- "Delete element" always, last.
- An element alone in a 1×1 grid therefore shows just "Edit text" and "Delete element" (pinned by e2e).

The component holds `useState<'edit-text' | 'edit-options' | 'delete' | null>` and renders the matching dialog, each with `finalFocus` pointing at the actions button. Handlers take the narrowed element (`TextCanvasElement | TextControlCanvasElement`, `DropdownCanvasElement`), as ADR 0002 rule 7 requires.

### `ElementPalette`

Each item is now a `<button type="button" aria-label="Add {label}">` inside its `<li data-uid>`, showing the label and `<code>&lt;{type}&gt;</code>`. Keep the `<code>`: the drag tests find the div and span items with `hasText: 'Div'` / `'Span'`, which now only match that tag text. The button has `focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-primary`.

The button is at once a drag handle and a menu trigger, and the two must not interfere:

- A click, Enter or Space opens the menu of cells; the first cell is focused on a keyboard open.
- A drag must not leave a menu open. Make the menu controlled (`open` / `onOpenChange`, one `openUid` in the palette) and close it from `useDndMonitor({ onDragStart })`. This holds whether Base UI's trigger opens on pointer down or on click, which the docs do not state.
- Picking a cell calls `addElement(item, cell, cell.elements.length)`; Base UI then closes the menu and returns focus to the button.

### `ThemePicker`

Base UI `Menu` with a `RadioGroup` whose value is the store's theme; each theme is a `RadioItem` (role `menuitemradio`, `aria-checked`). The popup carries `aria-label="Themes"`. The four-dot swatch is a small component used in the trigger and, wrapped in `data-theme={theme}`, in each row; the check icon is `invisible` unless the row is current.

- `RadioItem` needs `closeOnClick`: Base UI keeps the menu open after a radio pick by default, CDK closes it, and `theme.e2e.ts` asserts the menu is gone and the trigger focused after Enter.
- The trigger has `aria-describedby="theme-picker-current"`, pointing at a sibling `<span id="theme-picker-current" hidden>Current theme: {theme}</span>`. Without it the button tells the current theme only once its list is open.
- The list must open with the checked item **focused**, not merely scrolled into view: `theme.e2e.ts` asserts `toBeFocused()` on `fantasy` after a keyboard open and `toBeInViewport()` after a click. Base UI focuses the first item; the docs show no prop for the initial item, so focus the checked item from a ref when the menu opens.
- Typeahead must work ("dra" focuses `dracula`). Base UI matches on the item's text; pass `label={theme}` on each `RadioItem` so the swatch markup cannot get in the way.

### `LayoutPanel` (the one real behavioural trap)

React's `onChange` on an `<input>` is the native `input` event and fires per keystroke. The Angular code deliberately commits on native `change` so typing "12" does not pass through "1" and collapse the grid. In React: uncontrolled inputs (`defaultValue`, a `ref`), one `commit(axis, input)` on a native `change` listener attached through the ref (fallback: `onBlur` + Enter), then `show(axis, input)` writes `input.value` back from the store. `grid-layout.e2e.ts` ("applies a typed grid size once it is committed") guards this, and `keyboard.e2e.ts` sets the grid by typing, Tab and Enter, so Enter has to commit too.

## Dialogs

`TextInputDialog` and `DropdownOptionsDialog` become controlled Base UI `Dialog`s; `ConfirmationDialog` becomes a controlled `AlertDialog` (`@base-ui/react/alert-dialog`, role `alertdialog`), because it interrupts to ask about something that cannot be undone. All take `open` and `onOpenChange`. This replaces the `open*Dialog(dialog, options): Observable` helpers and removes RxJS. Each dialog's form body is a child component that mounts only while the dialog is open, so `useState(initialValue)` resets on every open (the `linkedSignal` equivalent) without a syncing effect.

Labelling: render the heading as `Dialog.Title` and the description as `Dialog.Description`, mounted only when a description is given. Base UI then references each from the popup, which is all `shared/dialog-labelling.ts` does by hand for CDK (`labelledDialogConfig`, `dialogTitleId`, `dialogDescriptionId`), so that file and its spec have nothing to port. The requirement it encodes still holds and is tested: the dialog is named by its visible heading, described by its visible description, and references no description when there is none (`toHaveAccessibleDescription` in `keyboard.e2e.ts`, "should be described by its description" in each dialog's test).

| Dialog | Props besides `open` / `onOpenChange` / `title` | Behaviour to keep |
|---|---|---|
| `ConfirmationDialog` | `description?`, `confirmLabel?` (default `'Confirm'`), `finalFocus?`, `onConfirm` | `alertdialog`; Cancel, the safe choice, has initial focus |
| `TextInputDialog` | `description?`, `value?`, `finalFocus?`, `onSubmit(text)` | textbox "Text" has initial focus; Save disabled while `!text.trim()`; Enter submits |
| `DropdownOptionsDialog` | `description?`, `options?`, `finalFocus?`, `onSubmit(options)` | see below |

`finalFocus` is the port of the new `restoreFocus` option: a ref to the element that takes focus when the dialog closes, passed straight to the `Popup`. When it is omitted, focus returns to whatever had it when the dialog opened.

`DropdownOptionsDialog` lives in the builder feature, not in `shared/`, because it edits a builder model type. It is a dynamic list of rows:

- State: `useState<Row[]>` where `Row = { id, text, value }`. The `id` (`crypto.randomUUID()`) exists only as the React `key`: index keys would move typed values and focus to the wrong row after a delete. Rows are stripped back to `{ text, value }` on submit, the same job `copyOption` does today.
- Starts with the given options, or one blank row when there are none. "Option 1 text" has initial focus.
- `invalid = rows.some((row) => !row.text.trim() || !row.value.trim())`; Save is disabled while invalid, and submit is a no-op.
- "Add option" appends a blank row and focuses its text input. "Delete option N" removes the row and focuses the previous row's text input, so focus does not fall out of the dialog. The first row's delete button is disabled.
- Labels: "Option N text", "Option N value", "Delete option N" (one-based).
- Never mutates the `options` prop (the spec passes frozen arrays).

## Styles and bundle size

- Tailwind's automatic source detection is off in the Angular app, because it scans the whole repository and every word in the docs and tests that names a daisyUI component ships that component. The same applies to a Vite project. `styles.css` starts:

  ```css
  @import 'tailwindcss' source(none);
  @source './**/*.{ts,tsx}';
  @source not './**/*.{spec,test}.{ts,tsx}';
  @plugin "daisyui" {
    themes: false;
  }
  ```

  Add `@source '../index.html'` only if `index.html` ever carries utility classes.
- The CDK imports (`overlay-prebuilt.css`, `a11y-prebuilt.css`) and the `.cdk-*` rules have no counterpart: Base UI positions its own popups, `sr-only` hides the live region, and the drag feedback is conditional classes.
- The Angular app loads `TextInputDialog` and `DropdownOptionsDialog` with `import()` on first use, because they are the only users of signal forms (about 60 kB). The React dialogs use `useState` and the same Base UI `Dialog` code the confirmation dialog already pulls in, so there is nothing comparable to defer. Import them statically; if the `vite build` report later shows a reason, `React.lazy` is the mechanism.

## Tests

- `vitest.config.ts` with two projects: `unit` (`src/**/*.spec.ts`, jsdom) and `components` (`src/**/*.test.tsx`, Chromium via `@vitest/browser-playwright`), reusing the two setup files.
- npm scripts keep the same names so the commands do not change: `test` → `vitest --project unit`, `test:components` → `vitest --project components`, `e2e` → `playwright test`.
- Store specs: `builder-store.spec.ts` and `theme-store.spec.ts` replace `TestBed.inject(...)` with the factories and signal reads with `store.getState()` / `selectCells(...)`. The `announcements` cases assert on the `vi.fn()` passed as `announce`. `createThemeStore` takes a detached document so the spec can seed `data-theme` and assert the attribute write and the appended stylesheet link.
- New `announcer.spec.ts`: creates the region once, writes the message, re-announces a repeated message.
- Component class-logic specs become pure-function specs or move to component tests:

| Angular spec | Goes to |
|---|---|
| `canvas.spec.ts` (`drop`) | `canvas-drop.spec.ts` for `resolveDrop` |
| `canvas.spec.ts` (`focusCell`, `focusElement`) | `canvas.test.tsx`, "keyboard focus" cases, which already exist on the Angular side |
| `dropped-element.spec.ts` (outline, `display`) | `dropped-element.spec.ts` for the `OUTLINE` and display mappings |
| `dropped-element.spec.ts` (editing, moving, deleting, `focusActions`, focus return) | `dropped-element.test.tsx` as observable behaviour |
| `element-palette.spec.ts` (`add` puts the item at the end of the cell) | `element-palette.test.tsx`; "lets nothing be dropped onto the palette" → a `resolveDrop` case |
| `dropdown-options-dialog.spec.ts`, `text-input-dialog.spec.ts`, `confirmation-dialog.spec.ts`, `layout-panel.spec.ts` | the component's `*.test.tsx`, asserting on the `onSubmit` / `onConfirm` callback prop, which the testing guide already allows for parent-supplied handlers |
| `dialog-labelling.spec.ts`, the `open*Dialog` helper cases | nothing to port |

- Component tests: `src/test/render-with-stores.tsx` wraps `render` in both store providers (and `DndContext` where needed) with preset state and a real announcer by default, so tests such as `theme-picker.test` "should announce the picked theme" can read the live region. Assertions in the existing `*.test.ts` files port with only the mount helper changed. Fixtures are built per element kind, never by spreading one kind into another (ADR 0002, "Costs").
- E2E: copy the six files. `playwright.config.ts` is the same except `webServer.command: 'npm run dev'` and port **5173** (not 4200), so `reuseExistingServer` can never attach to a running Angular dev server.
- Tests carry no comments, per `CLAUDE.md`.

